        <h3>English</h3>
        <p>Sunlight appears white, but it's actually a mix of all visible wavelengths (roughly 400–700 nm). When sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules (primarily N₂ and O₂) through a process called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>.</p>
        <p>Rayleigh scattering intensity is inversely proportional to the <strong>fourth power of wavelength</strong>:</p>
        <div class="formula">I ∝ 1/λ⁴</div>
        <p>This means shorter wavelengths (blue/violet, ~400–450 nm) are scattered roughly <strong>5–10× more strongly</strong> than longer wavelengths (red/orange, ~600–700 nm). So blue light gets redirected in all directions by air molecules, and when you look up, your eyes detect that scattered blue light coming from all parts of the sky.</p>
        <p><strong>Why not violet then?</strong> Violet light scatters even more than blue, but two factors make the sky appear blue rather than violet:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The Sun emits more blue light than violet light (its spectrum peaks in the visible at ~500 nm).</li>
          <li>Human cones are more sensitive to blue than violet, and some violet is absorbed in the upper atmosphere by ozone.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>At sunset, the path length through the atmosphere is much longer, so most of the blue has already been scattered out of the direct beam, leaving the redder wavelengths to reach your eyes.</p>
      </div>
