  <section class="section" id="phd">
    <div class="section-title">
      <h2>🔬 For a Physics PhD <span class="badge">给物理学博士</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="columns" data-en data-zh>
      <div class="card" data-lang="en">
        <h3>English</h3>
        <p>The short answer: <strong>elastic scattering by air molecules in the Rayleigh regime</strong>, with intensity ∝ 1/λ⁴.</p>

        <h4>Microscopic mechanism</h4>
        <p>Rayleigh scattering arises from the <strong>induced dipole</strong> interaction. An incoming electromagnetic wave with electric field <strong>E</strong> polarizes a molecule, inducing a dipole <strong>p = αE</strong>, where α is the molecular polarizability tensor. The scattered field from each molecule falls as 1/r (radiating dipole), and for a collection of N molecules in a volume V, the coherent/incoherent sum (molecular spacing ≫ λ) gives an intensity:</p>
        <div class="formula">I<sub>scat</sub> = (N²Vα²/r²) · 1/(2π)⁴ · ω⁴/c⁴ · (1 − cos²θ) · 2π sinθ dθ</div>
        <p>which simplifies (per unit solid angle) to the classical result:</p>
        <div class="formula">dσ/dΩ = λ⁻⁴ / 64π² · (2πn/N<sub>A</sub>)² · α² · (1 − cos²θ)</div>
        <p>For N₂ and O₂, the polarizabilities are small and nearly wavelength-independent over the visible, so the λ⁻⁴ dependence dominates.</p>

        <h4>Why the sky isn't violet</h4>
        <p>The incident solar spectrum follows (approximately) a Planck distribution with T ≈ 5800 K, so the photon flux at 400 nm is already lower than at 450 nm. Combined with the human luminous efficiency V(λ) (peaking near 555 nm), the product of incident spectral radiance × scattering cross-section × photopic sensitivity maximizes in the blue. Additionally, <strong>Hartley absorption</strong> (ozone, &lt;300 nm) further depletes the shortest visible wavelengths.</p>

        <h4>Why not red?</h4>
        <p>At solar zenith, the optical depth τ(λ) for Rayleigh scattering is ~0.1 (blue) to ~0.02 (red), so the attenuation factor e<sup>−τ</sup> is modest. However, over the longer chord length at low solar elevation (sec θ ≈ 10–20×), the blue optical depth becomes large enough that the direct solar beam is effectively blue-depleted, yielding the red/orange sunset.</p>

        <h4>Beyond Rayleigh: the full picture</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Mie scattering</strong> (aerosols, x = 2πr/λ ≳ 1) is nearly wavelength-independent and gives the whitish glow around the Sun.</li>
          <li><strong>Raman (inelastic) scattering</strong> contributes tiny non-resonant lines — negligible for color but measurable spectroscopically.</li>
          <li><strong>Multiple scattering</strong> and <strong>surface albedo</strong> add a diffuse component that slightly desaturates the blue.</li>
          <li>The <strong>depolarization ratio</strong> for linear molecules (N₂) is ~0.05–0.15 — relevant to remote sensing but visually negligible.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Bottom line</h4>
        <p>The blue color is a <em>kinematic</em> (λ⁻⁴) effect filtered through the <em>source</em> spectrum (Planck @ 5800 K), the <em>detector</em> (human cone response), and the <em>geometry</em> (path length). Remove any one factor and the color shifts — which is why Mars skies are butterscotch (CO₂ Rayleigh + Fe₂O₃ aerosols), Venus is pale yellowish (H₂SO₄ Mie), and a CO₂-only sky would look different.</p>
        <div class="refs">References: Rayleigh (1871/1872), Mie (1908), Bohren &amp; Huffman (1983), Seinfeld &amp; Pandis (2016)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card" data-lang="zh">
        <h3>中文</h3>
        <p>简短回答：<strong>空气分子在瑞利区（Rayleigh regime）下的弹性散射</strong>，强度 ∝ 1/λ⁴。</p>

        <h4>微观机制</h4>
        <p>瑞利散射源于<strong>诱导偶极</strong>相互作用。入射电磁波的电场 <strong>E</strong> 使分子极化，诱导偶极矩 <strong>p = αE</strong>，其中 α 为分子极化率张量。每个分子散射的场按 1/r 衰减（辐射偶极），对于体积 V 中 N 个分子，相干/非相干求和（分子间距 ≫ λ）给出强度：</p>
        <div class="formula">I<sub>scat</sub> = (N²Vα²/r²) · 1/(2π)⁴ · ω⁴/c⁴ · (1 − cos²θ) · 2π sinθ dθ</div>
        <p>化简后（按单位立体角）得到经典结果：</p>
        <div class="formula">dσ/dΩ = λ⁻⁴ / 64π² · (2πn/N<sub>A</sub>)² · α² · (1 − cos²θ)</div>
        <p>对于 N₂ 和 O₂，极化率很小且在可见光范围内几乎不随波长变化，因此 λ⁻⁴ 依赖性占主导地位。</p>

        <h4>为什么天空不是紫色的</h4>
        <p>入射太阳光谱近似为 T ≈ 5800 K 的普朗克分布，因此 400 nm 处的光子通量本身就低于 450 nm 处。结合人眼光视效率 V(λ)（峰值在 555 nm 附近），入射光谱辐射亮度 × 散射截面 × 明视觉敏感度这一乘积在蓝色区域取最大值。此外，<strong>哈特利吸收带</strong>（臭氧，&lt;300 nm）进一步耗竭了最短可见波长。</p>

        <h4>为什么不是红色？</h4>
        <p>在太阳天顶位置，瑞利散射的光学厚度 τ(λ) 约为 0.1（蓝）到 0.02（红），因此衰减因子 e<sup>−τ</sup> 并不大。然而在低太阳高度角下，穿过大气的弦长更长（sec θ ≈ 10–20倍），蓝光的光学厚度变得足够大，使得直射太阳光束中蓝光被有效耗竭，从而产生红色/橙色日落。</p>

        <h4>超越瑞利：完整图景</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>米氏散射</strong>（气溶胶，x = 2πr/λ ≳ 1）几乎不依赖波长，产生太阳周围的白色辉光。</li>
          <li><strong>拉曼（非弹性）散射</strong>贡献微小的非共振谱线——对颜色可忽略，但可通过光谱测量探测。</li>
          <li><strong>多次散射</strong>和<strong>地表反照率</strong>增加了漫射成分，使蓝色略微去饱和。</li>
          <li>线性分子（N₂）的<strong>去偏振比</strong>约为 0.05–0.15——对遥感相关，但对视觉可忽略。</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>总结</h4>
        <p>蓝色是一种<em>运动学</em>（λ⁻⁴）效应，经过<em>光源</em>光谱（5800 K 普朗克分布）、<em>探测器</em>（人眼视锥细胞响应）和<em>几何</em>（光路长度）的滤波。去掉其中任何一个因素，颜色都会改变——这正是火星天空呈黄褐色（CO₂ 瑞利散射 + Fe₂O₃ 气溶胶）、金星天空呈淡黄色（H₂SO₄ 米氏散射）的原因。</p>
        <div class="refs">主要参考文献：Rayleigh (1871/1872)，Mie (1908)，Bohren &amp; Huffman (1983)，Seinfeld &amp; Pandis (2016)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

